    plain: 'Low-dose aspirin is no longer treated as a painkiller. A 75 mg aspirin is heart protection, not an anti-inflammatory — but the audit recognised aspirin by name alone, so prescribing it alongside a common blood-pressure tablet raised a "painkiller plus blood-pressure drug, kidney risk" alert and took marks off the note. The audit now adds up the total daily aspirin dose on the prescription: at or below 100 mg a day it is treated as an antiplatelet only, and that alert no longer fires. Everything that should still fire, still does — aspirin with a second antiplatelet, aspirin with a blood thinner, and aspirin with a genuine anti-inflammatory all continue to alert, and full painkiller doses (650 mg three times a day, for instance) are unchanged. If the strength or frequency cannot be read at all, the aspirin is treated as antiplatelet — a missing strength is a documentation gap, not grounds for an alert, and the audit still raises the documentation gap separately. Scores RISE on affected notes; nothing can score lower because of this change, and no past audit is re-run. Separately, and affecting no score at all: the audit now quietly counts prescriptions that come CLOSE to a combination banned in India — one molecule extra, or one short — as an informational note, so we can measure whether checking for an exact match is too strict.',
